# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Six Pulses Across the Antenna Floor

**Story & Context:** During the first carrier test, Camila marks six small chalk strokes across the equipment-room floor and plays one tiple accent per mark. Tomás sequences a plain four-beat kick pattern underneath. The two systems line up only at specific points, yet neither changes tempo. Mateo reinforces the six-part phrasing with muted hand-drum taps while Irene waits until the third full cycle before entering with a low two-note bassoon figure.

Halfway through, Tomás removes the snare completely and leaves only kick and pulse-wave bass. Camila then changes the same tiple pattern from 3+3 emphasis to 2+2+2, making the groove appear to straighten without any sequencer edit. The final sixteen bars return to 3+3 while Irene sustains one low note through the reclassification, acting as the only continuous harmonic reference.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Six chalk marks on a grey concrete equipment-room floor beneath an old antenna frequency chart, Colombian tiple neck crossing the frame, bassoon keys reflecting green meter light, compact drum machine on a metal rack. No full lyrics; sparse spoken cues limited to “six across,” “same pulse,” “count it differently,” and “do not move the clock.”

## Track 2: Relay Three Opens After the Strum

**Story & Context:** Tomás discovers that one old mechanical relay in the rack produces a clean double-click whenever its manual test switch is pressed. The band assigns that click to the end of every eighth tiple phrase. Camila performs short muted chords that become progressively more syncopated, but the relay always answers at the same structural point.

Irene uses staccato bassoon notes in the spaces where the electronic bass has been shortened. Mateo plays only rim taps and fingertip strokes, refusing any full drum tone until the central section. On the fifth relay cycle, Tomás intentionally delays the click by one eighth-note. The entire band keeps playing, but the tiny displacement makes the grid feel briefly defective.

The final section restores the relay to its exact position and removes the synth bass entirely, leaving bassoon and tiple to carry the tonal center above the unchanged drum machine.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close documentary photograph of an old electromechanical relay labeled 3 with a manual test lever, tiple strings blurred in the foreground, bassoon reed and frame-drum rim nearby, cream frequency chart and oxidized rack screws behind. Spoken fragments limited to “relay three,” “after the strum,” “one eighth late,” and “put it back.”

## Track 3: Cloud Line Cuts the Carrier Tone

**Story & Context:** The EP closer begins with the twelve-second carrier-test tone used before early performances. The tone remains audible under the first tiple pattern while Tomás introduces only kick, one pulse-wave bass note, and dry snare. Outside the station, cloud gradually covers the ridge and the room darkens without any lighting change.

Camila lengthens her strums and leaves larger spaces between chord attacks. Irene shifts from staccato notes to breath-led bassoon pedals, while Mateo replaces hand-drum strokes with wood clicks taken from the relay cabinet. At the midpoint, the carrier tone is cut completely. No replacement drone enters. The sudden absence exposes how much of the listener’s tonal orientation had depended on a sound barely noticed before.

The final thirty-two bars run without the carrier, without reverb, and eventually without synth bass. The EP ends with one dry tiple chord, a bassoon key closing, and a single relay click after the room has gone silent.

